  "query_preview": "(This Evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 142 PM EDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nA progressively drier trend than the last couple weeks continues \nthrough much of the upcoming week as high pressure resides over the \nOhio Valley for much of the forecast period. Deep upper level \nridging will remain tethered for the next several days over the \nIntermountain West with a largely dry northwest flow over the \nregion. The passage of a cold front late Wednesday into Thursday \nwill present the primary convective risk until the latter part of \nnext weekend.\n\nRest of This Afternoon through Tuesday Night \n\nDiurnal cu development has increased with heating as temperatures \nwarm through the 70s. Satellite shows more enhanced cu over the \nGreat Lakes extending south into northern Indiana with a few showers \nnear KFWA in response to the double barreled upper low pinwheeling \nover Ontario. A piece of energy dropping south on the back side of \nupper low will pivot across lower Michigan and Lake Erie later this \nafternoon into the evening and may provide just enough lift in \ntandem with the steep lapse rates and weakly unstable airmass \npresent to generate stray showers over northeast counties into the \nearly evening. This would be similar to what took place Saturday \nevening but with lower instability levels limiting the coverage and \nintensity of any showers. Most will remain dry but any location that \ngets under a small shower may see a very quick downpour.\n\nHigh pressure will fully settle over the Ohio Valley tonight through \nmuch of the first half of the week with quiet weather and abundant \nsunshine anticipated. Low level thermals support cooler than normal \ntemperatures through Tuesday with highs generally ranging from the \nmid 70s northeast to the lower 80s in the lower Wabash Valley. Lows \nwill be very comfortable in the 50s the next two nights before \nreturn flow Tuesday night nudges lows back up into the low to mid \n60s for most of the forecast area. Really cannot ask for a better \nnext 60 hours or so for the region...much deserved after the very \nactive and wet last few weeks.\n\nWednesday through Sunday\n\nA weakening upper level low riding overtop of the western ridge will \ndrop into the upper Midwest early Wednesday before becoming absorbed \nby a broader long wave trough that will amplify across the eastern \nhalf of the country for late week. Low pressure and an associated \ncold front running in tandem with the upper wave will track across \nthe Ohio Valley Wednesday night and early Thursday in a \nprogressively weakened condition as stronger forcing aloft is lost. \nThere will be enough moisture and instability along and immediately \nahead of the frontal boundary for scattered convection with the \nfront clearing the forecast area during the day Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure will reestablish in the wake of the frontal passage \ndrifting slowly east through the Great Lakes into Saturday. This \nbring another multi day stretch of dry weather with seasonable \ntemperatures as highs will focus largely in the low to mid 80s. The \nridge will flatten to the west over the weekend opening the \npossibility for convection to impact the region by late weekend into \nearly next week as multiple waves ride along in the predominantly \nzonal flow aloft. Temperatures will also rise back into the mid and \nupper 80s by the end of the forecast period with an uptick in \nhumidity levels.",
